 ### Octree-GS Rendering
-Uses an octree structure to manage Gaussian visibility. It initializes a `GaussianModel` with dataset parameters, sets an anchor mask for the current view, prefilters voxels to determine visibility, and renders only the visible Gaussians [S8, unofficial].
+Uses an octree structure to manage Gaussians. It initializes `GaussianModel` with dataset parameters including `feat_dim`, `n_offsets`, `fork`, `use_feat_bank`, `appearance_dim`, `add_opacity_dist`, `add_cov_dist`, `add_color_dist`, `add_level`, `visible_threshold`, `dist2level`, `base_layer`, `progressive`, `extend` [S8, unofficial]. It applies a compatibility patch to `gaussian_renderer.generate_neural_gaussians` to replace `ape_code[0]` with `ape_code` [S8, unofficial]. It sets the anchor mask for the current view using `gaussians.set_anchor_mask(view.camera_center, scene.loaded_iter, view.resolution_scale)` [S8, unofficial]. It prefilters voxels to determine visibility using `prefilter_voxel(view, gaussians, pipeline, background)` [S8, unofficial]. It renders the scene using `render(view, gaussians, pipeline, background, visible_mask=visible, ape_code=args.ape)` with `ape_code` defaulting to 10 [S8, unofficial]. It clamps the rendered image to $[0.0, 1.0]$ and saves it [S8, unofficial]. It records the number of visible Gaussians per view in `per_view_count.json` [S8, unofficial].
 
 ### Unity 3DGS Rendering Pipeline Integration
-Integrates 3DGS into the Unity engine by inserting an additional sorting pass into each camera's command buffer and drawing calls for 3DGS objects into the transparent mesh queue based on bounding boxes. It avoids intermediate texture and color space conversion passes [S9, unofficial].
+Integrates 3DGS into the Unity engine. It inserts an additional sorting pass into each camera's command buffer [S9, unofficial]. It inserts draw calls for 3DGS objects into the existing rendering queue for transparent meshes based on their bounding boxes [S9, unofficial]. It differs from separate render texture approaches by avoiding an intermediate texture and color space conversion pass after alpha blending [S9, unofficial].
 
 ### Per-pixel Depth Comparison for Transparency
-Resolves popping artifacts caused by depth-sorting swaps by replacing global sorting with per-pixel depth comparison or ray-tracing [S10].
+A technique to resolve popping artifacts (temporal instability) caused by depth-sorting swaps by replacing global sorting with per-pixel depth comparison or ray-tracing [S10]. Popping artifacts arise from the depth-sorting step, where overlapping Gaussians may swap their sort order as the camera moves, causing a sudden discontinuity in alpha-blending accumulation [S10].
 
 ### Near-plane Culling
-A hardware-accelerated culling method using axis-aligned bounding boxes (AABB) to check depth and remove Gaussian points behind the camera near-plane. It checks $z_{\max} < z_{\text{near}}$ where $z_{\max} = z + \Delta z$ [S11].
+A culling method using axis-aligned bounding boxes (AABB) to check depth and remove Gaussian points behind the camera near-plane [S11]. It filters out Gaussian points that lie behind or too close to the near clipping plane before they enter the rendering pipeline [S11]. It checks each Gaussian's position relative to the camera near plane, discarding or skipping points that fail the test before projection [S11]. The culling condition is $z_{\max} < z_{\text{near}}$, where $z$ is the Gaussian center depth in camera coordinates, $\Delta z \ge 0$ is the half-extent of the AABB along the camera z-axis, $z_{\min} = z - \Delta z$, $z_{\max} = z + \Delta z$, and $z_{\text{near}}$ is the camera near-plane depth [S11]. Hardware unit design operates with a single MAC unit, latency of 4 cycles, processing inputs $(x, y, \text{depth})$ and view matrix row $[3:0]$, using D flip-flops (DFF) between operations for timing stability at 800 MHz FP16 [S11]. It achieves a 56% culling rate on the compressed model, saving 56% of unnecessary Gaussian point computations and memory accesses [S11]. It increases throughput from 15.81% to 35.94% (2.27× gain), raising FPS from 20.4 to 46.4 at 1080p [S11].
 
 ### Radiance Field Reconstruction from Gaussians
-Renders RGB, depth, alpha, and normal maps from a set of Gaussian splats. It extracts surface normals from rendered normals or depth, and estimates the scene bounding sphere [S12, unofficial].
+Reconstructs a radiance field representation by rendering RGB, depth, alpha, and normal maps from a set of Gaussian splats for a given set of viewpoints [S12, unofficial]. It initializes background color tensor: `background = torch.tensor(bg_color, dtype=torch.float32, device="cuda")` [S12, unofficial]. For each viewpoint in the stack, it calls the render function: `render_pkg = self.render(viewpoint_cam, self.gaussians)` [S12, unofficial]. It extracts RGB from render package: `rgb = render_pkg['render']` [S12, unofficial]. It extracts alpha from render package: `alpha = render_pkg['rend_alpha']` [S12, unofficial]. It computes surface normal by normalizing the rendered normal: `normal = torch.nn.functional.normalize(render_pkg['rend_normal'], dim=0)` [S12, unofficial]. It extracts depth: `depth = render_pkg['depth']` [S12, unofficial]. It extracts surface normal from depth: `depth_normal = render_pkg['surf_normal']` [S12, unofficial]. It appends the CPU tensors of RGB and depth to their respective lists: `self.rgbmaps.append(rgb.cpu())` and `self.depthmaps.append(depth.cpu())` [S12, unofficial]. After processing all viewpoints, it estimates the bounding sphere of the scene: `self.estimate_bounding_sphere()` [S12, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity**: $O(N)$ for projection and SH evaluation, where $N$ is the number of Gaussians. Rasterization is $O(N \cdot T)$ where $T$ is the number of tiles, but effectively linear with respect to visible Gaussians per pixel due to early termination [S5, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity**: $O(N)$ to store Gaussian parameters. The output image is $O(H \cdot W)$ [S1].
-*   **Performance**: Real-time performance on high-end GPUs (e.g., 100+ FPS at 1080p) is achievable with millions of Gaussians [S1]. On Apple Silicon via MLX, 100K Gaussians at 256x256 resolution take ~62.6ms for projection and ~29.8ms for covariance [S5, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths**: High rendering speed, differentiable, supports view-dependent appearance via SH [S1].
-*   **Limitations**: High memory usage for large scenes (gigabytes for millions of Gaussians), which can be problematic on low-end devices or VR/AR headsets [S1]. Sensitive to depth-sorting artifacts [S10].
-*   **When to Use**: Novel view synthesis, real-time rendering of static or dynamic scenes where high fidelity and speed are required.
-*   **When Not to Use**: Scenes with extremely high Gaussian counts on memory-constrained devices without compression, or when hardware rasterization of polygons is strictly required without integration [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The pipeline is dominated by the rasterization step, which is $O(N)$ for $N$ Gaussians, but with a constant factor dependent on tile size and overlap. The projection and covariance computation are also $O(N)$ [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** The scene representation requires storage for $N$ Gaussians, each with position, scale, rotation, opacity, and SH coefficients. Optimized scenes can consist of millions of Gaussians and require up to several gigabytes of storage and memory [S1].
+*   **Measured Performance:** On high-end GPUs, the pipeline shows real-time performance [S1]. On Apple Silicon with MLX, the full pipeline (forward pass) for 100K Gaussians at 128x128 resolution takes approximately 10-20ms (estimated from 1K/10K timings) [S5, unofficial]. Near-plane culling can increase FPS from 20.4 to 46.4 at 1080p [S11].
+*   **Scaling:** The pipeline scales well with resolution and number of Gaussians, but memory usage is a limiting factor on low-end devices [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Real-time rendering speeds, differentiable, explicit scene representation [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** High memory usage for large scenes, potential for popping artifacts due to depth sorting [S1, S10].
+*   **When to use:** Real-time novel view synthesis, VR/AR applications, interactive editing [S1].
+*   **When not to use:** Low-end devices with limited VRAM, applications requiring strict polygon integration without custom pipelines [S1].
 
 ## Background
-3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced by Kerbl et al. in 2023 [S1, S13]. It builds upon EWA volume splatting [S1] to efficiently compute the 2D footprint of projected Gaussians. The method models a scene as a set of 3D Gaussians with shape and appearance parameters, optimized via differentiable rendering to match recorded images [S1]. The rendering pipeline was designed to enable real-time performance on GPUs, contrasting with slower NeRF-based methods [S1]. Variants and ports include gsplat-mlx for Apple Silicon [S5, unofficial], Unity integration [S9, unofficial], and hardware accelerators [S11].
+3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced by Kerbl et al. in 2023 [S1]. It builds upon EWA volume splatting [S1]. The method models a scene as a set of 3D Gaussians with shape and appearance parameters, optimized via differentiable rendering to match a set of recorded images [S1]. The optimized scenes usually consist of millions of Gaussians [S1]. The pipeline was originally designed for CUDA GPUs [S1]. Ports to MLX (gsplat-mlx) have been developed for Apple Silicon [S5, unofficial]. Integrations with Unity [S9, unofficial] and hardware accelerators [S11] have also been developed.
 
